import std.fmt;
import std.io;

// `print` and its two renderings.
//
// `std.fmt` grants the rendering words; `std.io` grants the sink. Neither
// implies the other — drop either import and this file refuses at `Q015`, which
// is the point of having two modules rather than one `std` blob.
//
// `dec` prints a signed decimal `int32_t`; `hex` prints the same value as eight
// upper-case hexadecimal digits with an `0x` prefix. Both renderings are total
// on `int32_t` and on nothing else, so there is no width to get wrong and no
// format string to hold.
//
// Every operand is a scalar. `source.std-io.v1` has **no operand form that
// could denote a resource**, so nothing a `print` touches can be consumed,
// released or retained. That is why this route's derived effect row is exactly
// `io` — never `alloc`, never `mut`.
fn main() -> unit effects { io } {
    let total = 42;
    let mask = 255;
    print dec total;
    print hex mask;
    print dec -7;
    return;
}
